arXiv:2307.01140v2 [math.SG] 10 Nov 2023</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document"> Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dan Cristofaro-Gardiner and Richard Hind
</span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: August 24, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A finite volume symplectic manifold is said to have “packing stability” if the only obstruction to symplectically embedding sufficiently small balls is the volume obstruction. Packing stability has been shown in a variety of cases and it has been conjectured that it always holds. We give counterexamples to this conjecture; in fact, we give examples that cannot be fully packed by any domain with smooth boundary nor by any convex domain.
The examples are symplectomorphic to open and bounded domains in <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the diffeomorphism type of a disc.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">The obstruction to packing stability is closely tied to another old question, which asks to what extent an open symplectic manifold has a well-defined boundary; it follows from our results that many examples cannot be symplectomorphic to the interior of a compact symplectic manifold with smooth boundary. Our results can be quantified in terms of the volume decay near the boundary, and we produce, for example, smooth toric domains that are only symplectomorphic to the interior of a compact domain if the boundary of this domain has inner Minkowski dimension arbitrarily close to <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">The growth rate of the subleading asymptotics of the ECH spectrum plays a key role in our arguments. We prove a very general “fractal Weyl law”, relating this growth rate to the Minkowski dimension; this formula is potentially of independent interest.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Context</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The existence of symplectic ball packings, that is, symplectic embeddings of disjoint balls, has been understood as a basic measure of symplectic flexibility at least since the work of Gromov, see the discussion in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Sec. 1.1]</cite>.
This flexibility can be quantified by the <span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">packing numbers</span> <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> has finite volume we define</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M,\omega):=\sup_{c}\frac{k\mathrm{vol}(\overline{B(c)})}{\mathrm{vol}(M,\omega)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>c</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>vol</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M,\omega):=\sup_{c}\frac{k\mathrm{vol}(\overline{B(c)})}{\mathrm{vol}(M,\omega)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the supremum is over all <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> such that there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup\overline{B(c)}\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup\overline{B(c)}\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint closed balls.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, Ch. 6]</cite>, a theorem of McDuff and Polterovich <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, Rem. 1.5.G]</cite> shows that</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{k\to\infty}p_{k}(M,\omega)=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{k\to\infty}p_{k}(M,\omega)=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for all finite volume symplectic manifolds.
The volume filling limit is already a strong contrast with the Riemannian case. For example, for isometric ball embeddings into domains in <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, it has been known for ages that the limit of the analogous packing numbers is <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi/\sqrt{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mn>12</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi/\sqrt{12}</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> for a rigorous proof; for domains in <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding limit
is <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi/\sqrt{18}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mn>18</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi/\sqrt{18}</annotation></semantics></math> by celebrated work of Hales <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>; and, in any dimension, a general upper bound due to Rogers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> shows that the analogue of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1. Context ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) never holds.
We say that a connected symplectic manifold has <span id="S1.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">packing stability</span>
if there exists a (stability number) <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M,\omega)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M,\omega)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This represents perhaps a surprising level of flexibility, but packing stability has now been established for an ever increasing subset of symplectic manifolds. We say that a symplectic manifold <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.SS1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rational</span> if <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(M,\mathbb{Q})\subset H^{2}(M,\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(M,\mathbb{Q})\subset H^{2}(M,\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the manifolds shown to have packing stability include closed rational 4-manifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, closed rational manifolds of any dimension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, all closed 4 manifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, open ellipsoids <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, open 4 dimensional polydisks and pseudoballs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, and rational convex toric domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The question of determining which finite volume symplectic manifolds have packing stability was first raised in Biran’s seminal work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>; it is featured as Problem 4 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
Based on the results above, and the asymptotic estimate (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1. Context ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>), it was plausible to conjecture that
the answer is simply that all do.
Indeed, this is stated as Conjecture 13.2 in the survey article <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Main results</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now explain our main theorems. In some cases, we can prove more general statements than these, see Remark <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Remark 1.6. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>, but the theorems we present in this section get at the heart of our results while prioritizing brevity; we discuss how they fit together in Remark <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Remark 1.6. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first point of the present work
is to give examples of symplectic manifolds which do not have packing stability; our examples are open subsets of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. To state our result, recall that a <span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">toric domain</span> <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}\equiv\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>≡</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}\equiv\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of the form</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=X_{\Omega}:=\{(z,w)\,|\,(\pi|z|^{2},\pi|w|^{2})\in\Omega\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=X_{\Omega}:=\{(z,w)\,|\,(\pi|z|^{2},\pi|w|^{2})\in\Omega\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a
subset of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this paper, we will be interested in
toric domains <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}:=X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}:=X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is the
closed
subset of the first quadrant bounded by the axes and a convex function <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:[0,\infty)\to(0,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:[0,\infty)\to(0,b]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f(r)\,dr&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f(r)\,dr&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>; we call such a domain an <span id="S1.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unbounded concave toric domain</span>. We note that the function <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in the theorem does not have to be differentiable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> be an unbounded concave toric domain. Then
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f})</annotation></semantics></math> does not have packing stability. In fact, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(int(X_{f}))&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(int(X_{f}))&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> can not be fully filled, and so one would like to know what they look like. The <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> can be assumed to have smooth boundary, but since they are not compact this hides the behavior at infinity; we show below that they are symplectomorphic to interiors of compact domains, and it is more natural to study the boundary from this point of view. Then understanding the nature of the closure of the image of <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> connects to
a classic problem which goes back to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, of interest beyond the context of packing stability: to what extent does an open symplectic manifold have a well-defined boundary, and what symplectic properties of the manifold are determined by the boundary?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, we can give sharp results about the following facet of this.
In smooth topology, it is a fundamental problem to determine whether a given open manifold can be smoothly embedded as the interior of a smooth compact manifold with boundary: this is called the problem of “finding a boundary”, see for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. It is natural to ask the analogous question in the symplectic setting; prior to the present work, to our knowledge no symplectic obstructions to this problem have been known. Returning now to our domains <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>,
say that a function <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:[0,\infty)\to[0,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:[0,\infty)\to[0,b]</annotation></semantics></math> has <span id="S1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">asymptotic decay rate</span> <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> if</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{\ln f(x)}{\ln x}=-p." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{\ln f(x)}{\ln x}=-p.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For example, <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=(1+x)^{-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=(1+x)^{-p}</annotation></semantics></math> has asymptotic decay rate <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>: we denote the unbounded concave toric domain defined by this particular <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. We often call the asymptotic decay rate the <span id="S1.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decay rate</span> for brevity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has decay rate <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;p&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;p&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, and assume that
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f})</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to
a relatively compact subset <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of a symplectic manifold.
Then the inner Minkowski dimension of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Z</annotation></semantics></math> must be at least <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+2/p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+2/p</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f})</annotation></semantics></math> is not
symplectomorphic to the interior of any smooth compact symplectic manifold with piecewise smooth boundary.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the Minkowski dimension in section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1. Volume decay and the Minkowski dimension ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. The condition <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is required for the volume to be finite, and in fact the above theorem is actually optimal, as the following proposition shows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the manifold <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to the interior of a compact set in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with boundary of inner Minkowski dimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{max}(2+2/p,\,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn> 3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{max}(2+2/p,\,3)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note the
relation with the seemingly mysterious condition <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math>: when <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f})</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to a bounded domain with inner Minkowski dimension <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus the above <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+2/p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+2/p</annotation></semantics></math> bound is no longer optimal. We note that the Minkowski dimension is not in general a symplectic invariant: the domain <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> is also symplectomorphic to bounded sets of inner Minkowski dimension greater than <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+2p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The domains from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> give further examples of interesting packing stability phenomena, and this is the content of our final result. To elaborate, let us now turn to packings by more general domains. For example, the aforementioned Problem 4 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> asks for an investigation of packings by convex domains. To our knowledge, not many results were previously known about packings by domains that are not symplectomorphic to toric domains, but our techniques apply well in this case. To state one result, let <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z,\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z,\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic manifold and define the <span id="S1.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generalized packing number</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Z,k}(M,\omega):=\sup_{c}\frac{k\mathrm{vol}(Z,c\cdot\omega_{1})}{\mathrm{vol}(M,\omega),}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>c</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>vol</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Z,k}(M,\omega):=\sup_{c}\frac{k\mathrm{vol}(Z,c\cdot\omega_{1})}{\mathrm{vol}(M,\omega),}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where the supremum is over all <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> such that there exists a symplectic embedding of <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint copies of <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z,c\cdot\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z,c\cdot\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z,\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z,\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic manifold. Assume that either:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is compact with smooth boundary.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is convex in </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume in addition that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has asymptotic decay rate <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;p&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;p&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Z,k}(int(X_{f}))&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Z,k}(int(X_{f}))&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> have an interesting connection with the phenomenon known as “long-term superrecurrence”: this refers to situations where the images of any Darboux ball under iterates of a Hamiltonian diffeomorphism intersect more often than one would expect solely from volume considerations; for brevity, we refer the reader to the survey <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> for a fuller explanation.
To our knowledge, it had been unknown prior to the current work whether there are any natural symplectic manifolds <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> for which long-term superrecurrence occurs. It follows immediately from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> that this pheonmenon occurs whenever <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an unbounded concave toric domain <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> allows for a generalization of this, proving
long-term superrecurence for a large class of open subsets of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In combination Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> suggest the following perspective: the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> have provably complicated boundary, and this obstructs any full filling by a domain with qualitatively simpler boundary. A more general statement, which implies both of the aforementioned theorems, appears in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>. We further discuss this perspective in section <a href="#S5" title="5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As observed by S. Nemirovski <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, the domains <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> give examples of the following phenomenon: they are logarithmically convex and hence holomorphically convex, but are not biholomorphic to a bounded domain (as they contain copies of the complex plane <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>); on the other hand, in the symplectic category, it follows from our results that they are symplectomorphic to bounded domains but not to convex domains.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>The nature of the obstructions and the fractal Weyl law</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main idea in proving the above theorems is to study the ‘‘subleading asymptotics” of ECH capacities<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            One could alternatively use the subleading asymptotics of the “elementary ECH capacities” defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, and our proofs would hold verbatim for these as well, since they only rely on formal properties that are shared by both sets of capacities.</span></span></span> <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These are symplectic capacities, defined for any subset of a symplectic <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold; we summarize what we need to know about ECH capacities in section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. ECH capacities and concave toric domains ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. The leading asymptotics of these capacities were studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, with the conclusion (under some hypotheses that are not relevant to our discussion here, but which hold, for example, for any bounded open subset of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>) that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(X)\approx\sqrt{4vol(X)}\sqrt{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(X)\approx\sqrt{4vol(X)}\sqrt{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In other words, the <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> grow like <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the coefficient at highest asymptotics measures the volume of the manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Much less is currently understood about the subleading asymptotics</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X):=c_{k}(X)-2\sqrt{vol(X)k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X):=c_{k}(X)-2\sqrt{vol(X)k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are the invariants that we use to obstruct packing stability, and another consequence of our work is a better understanding of the <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> themselves. More precisely,
we can extract well-defined growth rates taking a wide range of values for bounded open connected subsets of <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, contrasting the case of the leading asymptotics.
To state our result, fix <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\geq p&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\geq p&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and recall <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the toric domain <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=(1+x)^{-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=(1+x)^{-p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1.8</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist positive constants <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> so that</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-C_{1}k^{1/2p}&lt;e_{k}(X_{p})&lt;-C_{2}k^{1/2p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-C_{1}k^{1/2p}&lt;e_{k}(X_{p})&lt;-C_{2}k^{1/2p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In view of the above proposition, it is natural to ask
to what degree the growth rate of the <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> sees information about a general finite volume domain <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, we can prove the following.
Define the <span id="S1.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ECH dimension</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ECH}:=2+4\limsup_{k\to\infty}\left(\frac{\ln(-e^{\prime}_{k}(Z))}{\ln(k)}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ECH}:=2+4\limsup_{k\to\infty}\left(\frac{\ln(-e^{\prime}_{k}(Z))}{\ln(k)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}_{k}(Z)=min(e_{k}(Z),0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}_{k}(Z)=min(e_{k}(Z),0)</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dim_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dim_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the inner Minkowski dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.9</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> be a relatively compact and open subset of a symplectic four-manifold. Then</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ECH}(Z)\leq dim_{M}(\partial Z)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ECH}(Z)\leq dim_{M}(\partial Z).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We can think of this as a “fractal Weyl law”, bounding the Minkowski dimension from below by a symplectic invariant, in analogy with formally similar fractal Weyl laws which abound in other areas of geometry, see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and the references therein.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4. </span>Outline of the paper and the main proofs</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now explain the structure of the paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reviews what we need to know about the
volume growth near the boundary of Riemannian manifolds, the Minkowski dimension, and ECH capacities, and collects some key preliminary results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The next two sections are about ECH capacities. Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> can be read as obstructions to symplectic embeddings between domains of equal volume, where, by the definition of packing numbers, we allow small rescalings of the domain. Since the ECH capacities of domains of equal volume have the same leading asymptotics, the subleading asymptotics can give obstructions. Section <a href="#S3" title="3. The subleading asymptotics of ECH capacities and the Minkowski dimension ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> establishes general lower bounds on the subleading asymptotics
for any symplectic manifold, via the aforementioned fractal Weyl law,
extending and quantifying an argument of Hutchings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Section <a href="#S4" title="4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> establishes upper bounds for unbounded concave toric domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this, we establish our main proofs in section <a href="#S5" title="5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Concave toric domains are shown to be symplectomorphic to bounded subsets of <math id="S1.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in section <a href="#S6" title="6. Boundedness. ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. The final section discusses packing stability in view of our new results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgements.</span> We would like to thank Dusa McDuff, Felix Schlenk and Richard Schwartz for reading a preliminary version of the text and providing very helpful comments. DCG also thanks the National Science Foundation for their support under agreement DMS-2227372, and RH thanks the Simons Foundation for their support under grant no. 633715. Key conversations about this project occurred during a visit by RH to the University of Maryland, hosted by the Brin Mathematics Research Center; we thank the center for their support. We also received valuable comments from the audience on an earlier version of this work that was presented by DCG at the “From smooth to <math id="S1.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> symplectic geometry” workshop at CIRM in July, 2023; we are very grateful to the organizers and to CIRM for their hospitality.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Volume decay and the Minkowski dimension</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by collecting some facts about the Minkowski dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our first order of business in this section involves clarifying some definitions. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be open, and assume that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial U</annotation></semantics></math> is compact. Recall the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Minkowski dimension</span> of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial U</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be defined as follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{V_{d}}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{V_{d}}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> be the volume of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{4}\,|\,\mathrm{dist}(x,\partial U)&lt;d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{4}\,|\,\mathrm{dist}(x,\partial U)&lt;d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We now define the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">upper Minkowski dimension</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dim_{M}(\partial U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dim_{M}(\partial U)</annotation></semantics></math> by the formula:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{dim}_{M}(\partial U)=4-\liminf_{d\to 0}\frac{\ln\hat{V_{d}}(U)}{\ln(d)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dim</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim inf</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{dim}_{M}(\partial U)=4-\liminf_{d\to 0}\frac{\ln\hat{V_{d}}(U)}{\ln(d)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">(There is also a lower Minkowski dimension, but this is not relevant to our results.)
The Minkowski dimension depends only on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial U</annotation></semantics></math>, and we will be interested in a slight refinement that is sensitive to <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> itself. Namely, define <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the volume of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x\in U\,|\,\mathrm{dist}(x,\partial U)&lt;d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\in U\,|\,\mathrm{dist}(x,\partial U)&lt;d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inner Minkowski dimension</span> in analogy by the formula</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{dim}_{M}(\partial X)=4-\liminf_{d\to 0}\frac{\ln V_{d}(U)}{\ln(d)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dim</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim inf</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{dim}_{M}(\partial X)=4-\liminf_{d\to 0}\frac{\ln V_{d}(U)}{\ln(d)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">It is immediate from the definition that the inner Minkowski dimension bounds the upper Minkowski dimension from below. It is clear that the inner Minkowski dimension is equivalent to the quantity</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\liminf_{d\to 0}\frac{\ln V_{d}(U)}{\ln(d)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim inf</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\liminf_{d\to 0}\frac{\ln V_{d}(U)}{\ln(d)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">which we call the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">order of the volume decay rate near <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial U</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will be interested in subsets of a general symplectic manifold <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> – this is the natural setting for Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>, for example — so we will want to generalize the above definitions. This is straightforward as long as <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset M</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in a compact set; recall that such a set is called <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">relatively compact</span>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> be a Riemannian metric on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset M</annotation></semantics></math> relatively compact and open; define <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d,g}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d,g}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> by copying the definition in the Euclidean case from above, but replacing the Euclidean metric with the metric <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following lemma states that the volume decay and thus the inner Minkowski dimension is independent of the choice of metric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> be an open and relatively compact subset of a smooth manifold <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be two Riemannian metrics on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\liminf_{d\to 0}\frac{V_{d,g}(U)}{d^{q}}&gt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim inf</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\liminf_{d\to 0}\frac{V_{d,g}(U)}{d^{q}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if and only if</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\liminf_{d\to 0}\frac{V_{d,g^{\prime}}(U)}{d^{q}}&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim inf</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\liminf_{d\to 0}\frac{V_{d,g^{\prime}}(U)}{d^{q}}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span>
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact set containing <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>; let <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the volume forms for <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively; let <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding distance functions, mapping <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times M</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=f\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=f\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for some smooth positive function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and similarly, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=ph^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=ph^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for some smooth positive function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:M\times M\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:M\times M\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. The function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) has strictly positive upper and lower bounds on <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. on <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\times K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\times K</annotation></semantics></math>).
Since by definition <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x\in Z|\mathrm{dist}_{g}(x,\partial Z)&lt;d\}\subset K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\in Z|\mathrm{dist}_{g}(x,\partial Z)&lt;d\}\subset K</annotation></semantics></math>, with the same fact true for <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the lemma follows.
 <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, we can define the inner Minkowski dimension and the volume decay for a bounded open subset of a general manifold <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> by choosing a metric <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and copying the definitions in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1. Volume decay and the Minkowski dimension ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1. Volume decay and the Minkowski dimension ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), replacing the quantity <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The next lemma proves what we need to know about the volume decay near piecewise smooth sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bounded domain whose boundary is
a finite union of compact subsets of smooth codimension <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> submanifolds of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a constant <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}(X)&lt;Cd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}(X)&lt;Cd</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span> Write the boundary as a union of compact subsets <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
of smooth submanifolds <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then points in within <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of the boundary of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the image of one of the exponential maps</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathrm{e}xp}_{S_{i}}:\nu^{d}S_{i}|_{C_{i}}\to\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathrm{e}xp}_{S_{i}}:\nu^{d}S_{i}|_{C_{i}}\to\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathrm{e}xp}_{S_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathrm{e}xp}_{S_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the exponential map of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{d}S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{d}S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the (rank <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>) normal disk bundle of radius <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. As the <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> have codimension <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> these sets are each
contained in
sets having volume of order <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">There is a similar result when the boundary is convex, even if it is not piecewise smooth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be an open and bounded convex domain. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Z</annotation></semantics></math> has volume decay of order <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span> The proof is a simple application of Steiner’s formula, which says in the case relevant here that for any convex body <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, the volume <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>
is given by the polynomial</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}(Z)=\sum^{4}_{i=0}Q_{i}(Z)r^{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}(Z)=\sum^{4}_{i=0}Q_{i}(Z)r^{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> are the “quermassintegrals”.
Now consider <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(d)=\{z\in Z\,|\,\mathrm{dist}(x,\partial Z)\geq d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(d)=\{z\in Z\,|\,\mathrm{dist}(x,\partial Z)\geq d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a convex domain, and since <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> can be alternatively described as the radius <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> tube around <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(d)</annotation></semantics></math>, the volume of <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z\in Z\,|\,\mathrm{dist}(x,\partial Z)&lt;d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z\in Z\,|\,\mathrm{dist}(x,\partial Z)&lt;d\}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}(Z(d))-N_{0}(Z(d))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}(Z(d))-N_{0}(Z(d)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.3" class="ltx_p">It is well-known that the quermassintegrals are Hausdorff continuous and positive, thus the lemma follows.
 <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>ECH capacities and concave toric domains</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> uses the subleading asymptotics of the ECH capacities <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, applied to a particular limit of concave toric domains. In this section, we briefly recall what we need to know about ECH capacities and concave toric domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The ECH capacities are a sequence of nonnegative real numbers <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> associated to four-dimensional symplectic manifolds; they were defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. One can think of ECH capacities as measurements of symplectic size. A key axiom that they satisfy is the Monotonicity Axiom, which states that each <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is monotone under symplectic embeddings. We also need to recall the Ball Axiom, which states that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(B(a))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(B(a))</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)^{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)^{st}</annotation></semantics></math> smallest entry in the matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ma+na)_{(m,n)\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\times\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ma+na)_{(m,n)\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\times\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the Disjoint Union axiom, which states that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(X\sqcup Y)=sup_{i+j=k}(c_{i}(X)+c_{j}(Y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊔</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(X\sqcup Y)=sup_{i+j=k}(c_{i}(X)+c_{j}(Y))</annotation></semantics></math> for compact four-dimensional Liouville domains <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The definition of ECH capacities arises from the “embedded contact homology” of closed three-manifolds with contact forms, and ECH capacities are most naturally defined for compact four-dimensional Liouville domains. However, in this paper we will need to consider slightly more wild objects. What we require for our purposes is that, as observed by Hutchings, the definition of ECH capacities extends to any open subset <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of a four-dimensional symplectic manifold by defining</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(M):=\sup_{Z}\hskip 2.84526ptc_{k}(Z)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>Z</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(M):=\sup_{Z}\hskip 2.84526ptc_{k}(Z),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact four-dimensional Liouville domain in <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, with many of the same properties holding. For more, we refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another important axiom about ECH capacities is the Volume Axiom, which states that for compact Liouville domains the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> recover the Volume in their limit; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> for the precise formula. Our obstruction to packing stability will come from the subleading asymptotics, calculated for the domains in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>. These domains are limits of concave toric domains and we now recall what we need to know about such domains from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. A <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">concave toric domain</span> is a toric domain <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is the subset of the first quadrant bounded by the axes and a convex function <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:[0,a]\to[0,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:[0,a]\to[0,b]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(a)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(a)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. (Recall in section <a href="#S1.SS2" title="1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> we defined an unbounded concave toric domain to be defined by a convex function <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\infty)\to(0,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\infty)\to(0,b]</annotation></semantics></math>.) For example, a ball is a concave toric domain. Any concave toric domain <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> has a canonical (possibly infinite) packing by balls <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, via the procedure explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Sec. 1.3]</cite>; we call the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weights</span> associated to <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>. To help the reader visualize the decomposition, we note that this packing comes from decomposing the region <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> into a union of triangles, each of which are equivalent under the action of the affine group to an isoceles right triangle; for more, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Fig. 1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">An important fact about concave toric domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> is that the weights determine the ECH capacities, via the formula:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{k}(X_{\Omega})=c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊔</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(X_{\Omega})=c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This formula is proved for concave toric domains such that the weight expansion is finite in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Thm. 1.4]</cite>, and then <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Rem. 1.6]</cite> explains how to extend this to arbitrary concave toric domains; only the finite case is needed as an input for this paper.
We will also need the following bound which immediately follows via induction from the definition of the weight sequence; it also follows as a special case of the more involved <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Lem. 3.6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> be a concave toric domain, such that the associated weights satisfy <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum a_{i}\leq M." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum a_{i}\leq M.</annotation></semantics></math>
Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{\Omega}\subset\{\pi|z_{1}|^{2}\leq M\}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega}\subset\{\pi|z_{1}|^{2}\leq M\}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>The subleading asymptotics of ECH capacities and the Minkowski dimension</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now begin the work towards the proofs of the main results.
Our main theorems all come from considerations of the subleading asymptotics of the ECH capacities.
The aim of this section is to prove the following general theorem, which is equivalent to the fractal Weyl law Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 1.9. ‣ 1.3. The nature of the obstructions and the fractal Weyl law ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.9</span></a>, but stated in a form that is more useful for our purposes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> be a relatively compact and open
subset of a symplectic manifold <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that the volume decay near <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Z</annotation></semantics></math> is of order <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a positive constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, depending only on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, such that
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(Z)&gt;-Ck^{(2-q)/4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(Z)&gt;-Ck^{(2-q)/4}</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One does not expect a similar upper bound in this level of generality; for example, even for smooth compact Liouville domains the ECH capacities could in principle be infinite, see for example the discussion after <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Thm. 1.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof makes use of the following proposition, which is a quantified variant
of Theorem 1.1 from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be an open
domain
and suppose there are constants <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}(X)&lt;Cd^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}(X)&lt;Cd^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a positive constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, depending only on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, such that
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(Z)\geq-Ck^{(2-q)/4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(Z)\geq-Ck^{(2-q)/4}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span> We follow closely the argument in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Thm. 1.1]</cite>, which essentially deals with the case <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(a,a)=X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(a,a)=X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> be a polydisc: this corresponds to the case where <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a square of side lengths <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> with legs on the axes. One first estimates the subleading asymptotics <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of a polydisc by using the fact that the ECH capacities in this case have an explicit combinatorial formula, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Lem. 4.1]</cite>, with the conclusion that <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(P(a,a))\geq-2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(P(a,a))\geq-2a</annotation></semantics></math>. The polydiscs <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(a,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(a,a)</annotation></semantics></math> are symplectomorphic to cubes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now fill the interior of <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with open cubes, by making use of the lattice <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{-n}\mathbb{Z}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{-n}\mathbb{Z}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows. First, we add in all open cubes contained in <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with vertices on neighboring <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{-1}\mathbb{Z}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{-1}\mathbb{Z}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> lattice points. Next, we add in all open cubes contained in <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with vertices on neighboring <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{-2}\mathbb{Z}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{-2}\mathbb{Z}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> lattice points that do not intersect the cubes from the previous step. We continue inductively in this way, getting at the <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> step an embedding into <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of a disjoint union of a finite number of (open) cubes, each of volume <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{-4n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{-4n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Now we apply <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Lemma 4.2]</cite>, which says the following. (The lemma was originally stated for bounded domains, but this assumption is not used in the proof.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose there exists a symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup P_{i}\hookrightarrow X" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup P_{i}\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a copy of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(a_{i},a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(a_{i},a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k}=\{i\,|\,a_{i}^{2}\geq\mathrm{vol}(X)/k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k}=\{i\,|\,a_{i}^{2}\geq\mathrm{vol}(X)/k\}</annotation></semantics></math> and let</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{k}=\mathrm{vol}\left(\cup_{i\in I_{k}}P_{i}\right)=\sum_{i\in I_{k}}a_{i}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>vol</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k}=\mathrm{vol}\left(\cup_{i\in I_{k}}P_{i}\right)=\sum_{i\in I_{k}}a_{i}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="e_{k}(X)\geq-2\sqrt{2}\sum_{i\in I_{k}}a_{i}+2\frac{(V_{k}-\mathrm{vol}(X))}{\sqrt{\mathrm{vol}(X)}}\sqrt{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)\geq-2\sqrt{2}\sum_{i\in I_{k}}a_{i}+2\frac{(V_{k}-\mathrm{vol}(X))}{\sqrt{\mathrm{vol}(X)}}\sqrt{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Returning to our situation, let <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of cubes constructed in the first <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> steps. We see that <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus X_{n}\subset N_{2^{1-n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus X_{n}\subset N_{2^{1-n}}</annotation></semantics></math> and so has volume at most <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C2^{-qn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C2^{-qn}</annotation></semantics></math>. As the cubes added in the <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> step each have volume <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{-4n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{-4n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we see the number added is bounded above by <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C2^{n(4-q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C2^{n(4-q)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Now suppose</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16^{n}\leq\frac{k}{\mathrm{vol}(X)}&lt;16^{n+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>16</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>16</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16^{n}\leq\frac{k}{\mathrm{vol}(X)}&lt;16^{n+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly the indices corresponding to the cubes added in the first <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\in I_{k}}a_{i}\leq\sum_{j=1}^{n}C2^{j(4-q)}2^{-2j}&lt;C2^{n(2-q)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\in I_{k}}a_{i}\leq\sum_{j=1}^{n}C2^{j(4-q)}2^{-2j}&lt;C2^{n(2-q)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p11.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k}-\mathrm{vol}(X)&gt;-C2^{-qn}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k}-\mathrm{vol}(X)&gt;-C2^{-qn}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p11.3" class="ltx_p">Therefore the lemma gives</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X)\geq-Ck^{(2-q)/4}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X)\geq-Ck^{(2-q)/4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p11.4" class="ltx_p">as required.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove the main theorem from this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span>(<span id="S3.p14.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3. The subleading asymptotics of ECH capacities and the Minkowski dimension ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a></span>)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is relatively compact, it is contained in a compact set <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is compact, we can cover it with finitely many compact Darboux balls <math id="S3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\ldots,B_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\ldots,B_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with domain centered at the origin in <math id="S3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>; we identify these balls with their image. The union of the <math id="S3.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a
refinement into a collection of compact sets <math id="S3.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq N</annotation></semantics></math>,
with disjoint interiors: let <math id="S3.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of the <math id="S3.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set obtained by subtracting all of the boundaries of the <math id="S3.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, and then take the <math id="S3.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the closures of the connected components of <math id="S3.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}.</annotation></semantics></math>
(Here, by a refinement we mean that each <math id="S3.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in a <math id="S3.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the union of the <math id="S3.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the union of the <math id="S3.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.)
We identify each <math id="S3.p15.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with its image in <math id="S3.p15.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Now consider <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{j}=int(Z\cap S_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{j}=int(Z\cap S_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. This is symplectomorphic to an open and bounded subset <math id="S3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}^{4},\omega_{std})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}^{4},\omega_{std})</annotation></semantics></math>. The identification of <math id="S3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with a subset of <math id="S3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> induces a decomposition</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Q_{i}=X_{reg}\cup X_{Z}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Q_{i}=X_{reg}\cup X_{Z},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p16.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{reg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{reg}</annotation></semantics></math> are those points that correspond to points on the boundary of some <math id="S3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> are those points that correspond to points in <math id="S3.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Z\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Z\subset M</annotation></semantics></math>. Letting <math id="S3.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}(V)\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}(V)\subset V</annotation></semantics></math> be the points within distance <math id="S3.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial V</annotation></semantics></math>, we therefore have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}(\partial Q_{i})=N_{d}(X_{reg})\cup N_{d}(X_{Z})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}(\partial Q_{i})=N_{d}(X_{reg})\cup N_{d}(X_{Z}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p16.3" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S3.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{reg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{reg}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in a finite union of compact hypersurfaces, since its image in <math id="S3.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the union of the boundaries of the <math id="S3.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.1. Volume decay and the Minkowski dimension ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> the volume of <math id="S3.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}(X_{reg})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}(X_{reg})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d)</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, by the argument in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Volume decay and the Minkowski dimension ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, the volume of <math id="S3.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}(X_{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}(X_{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is, up to a constant, bounded by the volume of the tube <math id="S3.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}(\partial Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}(\partial Z)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and thus <math id="S3.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d^{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d^{q})</annotation></semantics></math> by assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(Z)\geq c_{k}(\sqcup\Delta_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊔</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(Z)\geq c_{k}(\sqcup\Delta_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p17.2" class="ltx_p">and we estimate the right hand side using the Disjoint Union Axiom.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(\sqcup\Delta_{j})=\sup_{i_{1}+\dots+i_{N}=k}\sum c_{i_{j}}(\Delta_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊔</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(\sqcup\Delta_{j})=\sup_{i_{1}+\dots+i_{N}=k}\sum c_{i_{j}}(\Delta_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p">By choosing <math id="S3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}=\lfloor k\frac{vol(\Delta_{j})}{vol(Z)}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}=\lfloor k\frac{vol(\Delta_{j})}{vol(Z)}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3. The subleading asymptotics of ECH capacities and the Minkowski dimension ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> gives</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{i_{1}+\dots+i_{N}=k}\sum c_{i_{j}}(\Delta_{j})\geq\sum 2\sqrt{k\frac{vol(\Delta_{j})^{2}}{vol(Z)}-\epsilon_{j}vol(\Delta_{j})}-C_{j}i_{j}^{(2-q)/4}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{i_{1}+\dots+i_{N}=k}\sum c_{i_{j}}(\Delta_{j})\geq\sum 2\sqrt{k\frac{vol(\Delta_{j})^{2}}{vol(Z)}-\epsilon_{j}vol(\Delta_{j})}-C_{j}i_{j}^{(2-q)/4},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p18.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\epsilon_{j}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\epsilon_{j}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is the fractional part of <math id="S3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\frac{vol(\Delta_{j})}{vol(Z)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\frac{vol(\Delta_{j})}{vol(Z)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Continuing, the right hand side in the above inequality is bounded from below by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\left(2\sqrt{k\frac{vol(\Delta_{j})^{2}}{vol(Z)}}-2\sqrt{\epsilon_{j}vol(\Delta_{j})}-C_{j}i_{j}^{(2-q)/4}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\left(2\sqrt{k\frac{vol(\Delta_{j})^{2}}{vol(Z)}}-2\sqrt{\epsilon_{j}vol(\Delta_{j})}-C_{j}i_{j}^{(2-q)/4}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=2\sqrt{kvol(Z)}-\sum\left(2\sqrt{\epsilon_{j}vol(\Delta_{j})}+C_{j}i_{j}^{(2-q)/4}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=2\sqrt{kvol(Z)}-\sum\left(2\sqrt{\epsilon_{j}vol(\Delta_{j})}+C_{j}i_{j}^{(2-q)/4}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 2\sqrt{kvol(Z)}-2Nvol(Z)-Ck^{(2-q)/4}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 2\sqrt{kvol(Z)}-2Nvol(Z)-Ck^{(2-q)/4},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p19.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\sum C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\sum C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, since</p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum C_{j}i_{j}^{(2-q)/4}\leq(\sum C_{j})max_{j}(i_{j})^{(2-q)/4}\leq(\sum C_{j})(\sum i_{j})^{(2-q)/4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum C_{j}i_{j}^{(2-q)/4}\leq(\sum C_{j})max_{j}(i_{j})^{(2-q)/4}\leq(\sum C_{j})(\sum i_{j})^{(2-q)/4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p19.3" class="ltx_p">The required bound for <math id="S3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> follows immediately.
 <math id="S3.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The previous section gave general lower bounds on the subleading asymptotics of any relatively compact open subset.
In this section, we aim to prove the results we will need about unbounded concave toric domains, which will be an essentially precise calculation of the subleading asymptotics. More precisely, we aim to prove the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be an unbounded concave toric domain. Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{k\to\infty}e_{k}(\text{int}(X))=-\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">int</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{k\to\infty}e_{k}(\text{int}(X))=-\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the case when <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by a convex function <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with decay rate <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;p\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;p\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
there exists a constants <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-C_{1}k^{1/2p+\epsilon}&lt;e_{k}(X_{p})&lt;-C_{2}k^{1/2p-\epsilon}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-C_{1}k^{1/2p+\epsilon}&lt;e_{k}(X_{p})&lt;-C_{2}k^{1/2p-\epsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The proof of the upper bound in the above proposition does not use the condition <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math> at all; however, we do not need this result for any of the applications in our paper.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>The weight expansion and the ECH capacities</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by generalizing the relationship between the ECH capacities and the weight sequence for concave toric domains to the case of unbounded concave toric domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To shorten the notation, denote any unbounded concave toric domain <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>
by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X.</annotation></semantics></math>
To prove the theorem, it is helpful to approximate <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by concave toric domains <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows. The definition of the weight sequence in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Sec. 1.3]</cite> extends verbatim to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, even though it is infinite, since the function <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is convex. Thus we obtain a sequence of weights <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, which also have the property that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\ldots,a_{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\ldots,a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">are the weights for some concave toric domain; we denote this concave toric domain by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is some flexibility in how to order the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; we assume that they are in nonincreasing order.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that by definition,</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}\subset X_{2}\ldots\subset X_{n}\subset\ldots" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}\subset X_{2}\ldots\subset X_{n}\subset\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The following lemma explains what we need to know regarding how the domains <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> approximate <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="int(X)=\cup_{n}int(X_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X)=\cup_{n}int(X_{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span>
An equivalent description of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\cup_{n}int(X_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cup_{n}int(X_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, that follows from the definition of the weight expansion, is that <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\cup_{n}int(X_{n})=X_{\Omega^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cup_{n}int(X_{n})=X_{\Omega^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the union of all triangles
bounded by the axes and a<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            Recall that by a <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tangent line</span> in this possibly non-smooth, but convex, setting, we mean a straight line through a point on the graph of <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> whose intersection with the first quadrant stays entirely in the region bounded by the axes and the graph of <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> tangent line to the graph of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with rational slope, with the vertical and horizontal axes contained in <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> but not the hypotenuse.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It follows from the definitions that <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega^{\prime}}\subset int(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega^{\prime}}\subset int(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. To see the opposite inclusion, given any point <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in int(X_{\Omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in int(X_{\Omega})</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the corresponding point in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>, and choose a point <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math> on the graph of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;b</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the triangle bounded by the axes and any tangent line to the graph of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math>, and is not on the hypotenuse. If there is a tangent line at <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math> with rational slope then the corresponding triangle lies in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and we are done; if it does not, we can
find a point <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c^{\prime},d^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c^{\prime},d^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> on the graph of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> which has a tangent line of rational slope sufficiently close to the irrational slope at <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding triangle will still contain <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We can now extend (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2. ECH capacities and concave toric domains ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>) to our manifold <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a limit of concave toric domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{k}(int(X))=c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊔</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(int(X))=c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span> By the Monotonicity Property of ECH capacities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, we have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{k}(int(X))\geq c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊔</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(int(X))\geq c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">so we have to establish the opposite inequality. By definition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Defn. 4.9]</cite>,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(int(X)):=\sup_{Z}\hskip 2.84526ptc_{k}(Z)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>Z</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(int(X)):=\sup_{Z}\hskip 2.84526ptc_{k}(Z),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact Liouville domain in <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> embeds into some <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, assume the opposite, and define a sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{n}\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{n}\in Z</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is not in <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>; pass to a subsequence converging to some <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\infty}\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\infty}\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>; the point <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, hence by Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4.1. The weight expansion and the ECH capacities ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> it is in <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a contradiction in view of (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.1. The weight expansion and the ECH capacities ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Thus we have <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(Z)\leq c_{k}(X_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(Z)\leq c_{k}(X_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, by (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2. ECH capacities and concave toric domains ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>), <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{k}(X_{n})=c_{k}(\sqcup^{n}_{i=1}B(a_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(X_{n})=c_{k}(\sqcup^{n}_{i=1}B(a_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> and so we have that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{k}(Z)\leq c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊔</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(Z)\leq c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sup_{Z}\hskip 2.84526ptc_{k}(Z)\leq c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mi>Z</mi></munder><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊔</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{Z}\hskip 2.84526ptc_{k}(Z)\leq c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.3" class="ltx_p">and so the lemma is proved.
 <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The following is a useful method to estimate weights of concave toric domains, including the unbounded case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{\prime}(x)=-1/i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}(x)=-1/i</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\geq f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\geq f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span> It suffices to find <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> weights <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> all satisfying <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}\geq f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}\geq f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from the definition of the weight expansion that for each positive integer <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a weight for the associated toric domain corresponding to an affine triangle whose slant edge meets the graph of <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> at the point <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{\prime}(x)=-1/n;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}(x)=-1/n;</annotation></semantics></math> moreover, it follows from the definition of the weight expansion that this weight is at least as large as <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. The result follows.
 <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Estimating the subleading asymptotics in terms of the weights</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove the first part of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>; we also a prove a useful upper bound for the subleading asymptotics in terms of the weights.
Throughout <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> will denote a, possibly varying, positive constant, which is independent of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is unbounded, then</span></p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{k\to\infty}e_{k}(\mathrm{int}(X))=-\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{k\to\infty}e_{k}(\mathrm{int}(X))=-\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\geq C\left(\frac{1}{i}\right)^{\frac{p}{p+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\geq C\left(\frac{1}{i}\right)^{\frac{p}{p+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(\mathrm{int}(X))\leq-Ck^{\frac{1}{2p}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>int</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(\mathrm{int}(X))\leq-Ck^{\frac{1}{2p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span> By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4.1. The weight expansion and the ECH capacities ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, it suffices to prove the same result for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊔</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(\sqcup_{i}B(a_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(k)=\{d(k)_{i}\}_{i=1,\ldots}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(k)=\{d(k)_{i}\}_{i=1,\ldots}</annotation></semantics></math> be a maximizer over the nonnegative integers <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, of the optimization problem</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\sum_{i}d_{i}a_{i}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526pt\sum_{i}(d_{i}^{2}+d_{i})\leq 2k\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\sum_{i}d_{i}a_{i}\hskip 2.84526pt|\hskip 2.84526pt\sum_{i}(d_{i}^{2}+d_{i})\leq 2k\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">noting that such a maximum does exist and can be assumed to have the properties that <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(k)_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(k)_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonincreasing in <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(k)_{j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(k)_{j}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}d(k)_{i}^{2}+d(k)_{i}=2k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}d(k)_{i}^{2}+d(k)_{i}=2k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The reason we are interested in the <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(k)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(k)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is that by the Disjoint Union <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Prop. 1.5]</cite> and Ball <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Cor. 1.3]</cite> axioms reviewed above, we have that</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}a_{i}d(k)_{i}=c_{k}(M);" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}a_{i}d(k)_{i}=c_{k}(M);</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Sec. 1.1]</cite> for further discussion of the infinite case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now</p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}(d(k)^{2}_{i}+d(k)_{i})=2k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}(d(k)^{2}_{i}+d(k)_{i})=2k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Applying Cauchy-Schwarz we obtain</p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}a_{i}\sqrt{d(k)^{2}_{i}+d(k)_{i}}\leq\sqrt{2vol}\sqrt{2k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}a_{i}\sqrt{d(k)^{2}_{i}+d(k)_{i}}\leq\sqrt{2vol}\sqrt{2k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{vol}:=\mathrm{vol}(X)=\frac{1}{2}\sum a_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{vol}:=\mathrm{vol}(X)=\frac{1}{2}\sum a_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">By invoking (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In 4.2. Estimating the subleading asymptotics in terms of the weights ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>) and Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4.1. The weight expansion and the ECH capacities ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, we can rewrite the above inequality as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="e_{k}(\mathrm{int}(X))=c_{k}(\sqcup B(a_{i}))-2\sqrt{k\mathrm{vol}}\leq-\sum_{i}a_{i}\left(\sqrt{d(k)^{2}_{i}+d(k)_{i}}-d(k)_{i}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>int</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊔</mo><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>vol</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(\mathrm{int}(X))=c_{k}(\sqcup B(a_{i}))-2\sqrt{k\mathrm{vol}}\leq-\sum_{i}a_{i}\left(\sqrt{d(k)^{2}_{i}+d(k)_{i}}-d(k)_{i}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(k)</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimum <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(k)_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(k)_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The function <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\sqrt{n^{2}+n}-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\sqrt{n^{2}+n}-n</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonnegative, strictly increasing function on the domain <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so by above,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="e_{k}(\mathrm{int}(X))\leq-(\sqrt{2}-1)\sum^{I(k)-1}_{i}a_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>int</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(\mathrm{int}(X))\leq-(\sqrt{2}-1)\sum^{I(k)-1}_{i}a_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4. ‣ 2.2. ECH capacities and concave toric domains ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, to show (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proposition 4.6. ‣ 4.2. Estimating the subleading asymptotics in terms of the weights ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), it suffices to show that</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{k\to\infty}I(k)=+\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{k\to\infty}I(k)=+\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.3" class="ltx_p">As for (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Proposition 4.6. ‣ 4.2. Estimating the subleading asymptotics in terms of the weights ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>), if we assume that
<math id="S4.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\geq C\left(\frac{1}{i}\right)^{\frac{p}{p+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\geq C\left(\frac{1}{i}\right)^{\frac{p}{p+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then we have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-(\sqrt{2}-1)\sum^{I(k)-1}_{i}a_{i}\leq-C\int^{I(k)}_{1}\frac{1}{x^{p/(p+1)}}dx" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>C</mi><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-(\sqrt{2}-1)\sum^{I(k)-1}_{i}a_{i}\leq-C\int^{I(k)}_{1}\frac{1}{x^{p/(p+1)}}dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=-C(I(k)^{\frac{1}{p+1}}-1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=-C(I(k)^{\frac{1}{p+1}}-1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.4" class="ltx_p">so that it suffices to prove that</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(k)\geq Ck^{\frac{p+1}{2p}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(k)\geq Ck^{\frac{p+1}{2p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We find the needed lower bounds on <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(k)</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;I(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;I(k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we claim that</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{j}+a_{I(k)}+\ldots+a_{I(k)+d(k)_{j}-1}\leq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{j}+a_{I(k)}+\ldots+a_{I(k)+d(k)_{j}-1}\leq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Indeed, if we consider a sequence of integers <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}(k)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}(k)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (indexed by <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>) that is identical to <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(k)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(k)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> except that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}(k)_{j}=d(k)_{j}-1,\quad\quad d^{\prime}(k)_{I(k)}=\ldots=d^{\prime}(k)_{I(k)+d(k)_{j}-1}=1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}(k)_{j}=d(k)_{j}-1,\quad\quad d^{\prime}(k)_{I(k)}=\ldots=d^{\prime}(k)_{I(k)+d(k)_{j}-1}=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.3" class="ltx_p">then the <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}(d(k)_{i}^{\prime 2}+d(k)_{i}^{\prime})=\sum_{i}(d_{i}(k)^{2}+d(k)_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}(d(k)_{i}^{\prime 2}+d(k)_{i}^{\prime})=\sum_{i}(d_{i}(k)^{2}+d(k)_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the claimed inequality follows immediately from the fact that the <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(k)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(k)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are maximizers of the optimization problem (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In 4.2. Estimating the subleading asymptotics in terms of the weights ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Now (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>), applied to <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math>, implies (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.2. Estimating the subleading asymptotics in terms of the weights ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>). Otherwise, there is a subsequence with <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(k)</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly bounded, and necessarily on this subsequence <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(k)_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(k)_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is unbounded. But then the divergence of <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a contradiction (as <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, it remains to prove (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.2. Estimating the subleading asymptotics in terms of the weights ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>) under the assumption that <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\geq C\left(\frac{1}{i}\right)^{\frac{p}{p+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\geq C\left(\frac{1}{i}\right)^{\frac{p}{p+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and this will be our standing assumption for the rest of the proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>) and the fact that the <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(k_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(k_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> are nonincreasing, we have that <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{j}+d(k)_{j}a_{I+d(k)_{j}-1}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{j}+d(k)_{j}a_{I+d(k)_{j}-1}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and so</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(k)_{j}\leq\frac{a_{j}}{a_{I(k)+d(k)_{j}-1}}\leq\frac{a_{j}}{a_{I(k)+\lceil\sqrt{2k}\rceil}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(k)_{j}\leq\frac{a_{j}}{a_{I(k)+d(k)_{j}-1}}\leq\frac{a_{j}}{a_{I(k)+\lceil\sqrt{2k}\rceil}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.2" class="ltx_p">where in the last inequality we have used the fact that <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(k)_{j}\leq\sqrt{2k}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(k)_{j}\leq\sqrt{2k}+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Hence, in view of the inequality above, the identity (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.2. Estimating the subleading asymptotics in terms of the weights ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>), and the definition of <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k=\sum^{I(k)}_{j=1}d(k)^{2}_{j}+d(k)_{j}\leq\sum^{I(k)}_{j=1}2d(k)^{2}_{j}\leq 2\sum^{I(k)}_{j=1}\frac{a^{2}_{j}}{a^{2}_{I(k)+\lceil\sqrt{2k}\rceil}}\leq\frac{4vol}{a^{2}_{I(k)+\lceil\sqrt{2k}\rceil}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k=\sum^{I(k)}_{j=1}d(k)^{2}_{j}+d(k)_{j}\leq\sum^{I(k)}_{j=1}2d(k)^{2}_{j}\leq 2\sum^{I(k)}_{j=1}\frac{a^{2}_{j}}{a^{2}_{I(k)+\lceil\sqrt{2k}\rceil}}\leq\frac{4vol}{a^{2}_{I(k)+\lceil\sqrt{2k}\rceil}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.2" class="ltx_p">It follows that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{2}_{I(k)+\lceil\sqrt{2k}\rceil}\leq\frac{4\mathrm{vol}}{2k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{2}_{I(k)+\lceil\sqrt{2k}\rceil}\leq\frac{4\mathrm{vol}}{2k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.3" class="ltx_p">hence</p>
<table id="S4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I(k)+\sqrt{2k}+1)^{2p/(p+1)}\geq Ck." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I(k)+\sqrt{2k}+1)^{2p/(p+1)}\geq Ck.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">The inequality (<a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.2. Estimating the subleading asymptotics in terms of the weights ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>) follows from this since <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2&lt;(p+1)/(2p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2&lt;(p+1)/(2p)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Subleading asymptotics and decay rates</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we obtain tight bounds for the subleading asymptotics of concave domains in terms of their decay rates, and state precise bounds in the case of the <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, see Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem10" title="Proposition 4.10. ‣ 4.3. Subleading asymptotics and decay rates ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>. These bounds are an application of the lower bounds coming from section <a href="#S3" title="3. The subleading asymptotics of ECH capacities and the Minkowski dimension ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and the upper bounds from section <a href="#S4" title="4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall we suppose <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is convex with decay rate <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;p\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;p\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, that is,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{\ln f(x)}{\ln x}=-p." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{\ln f(x)}{\ln x}=-p.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will prove the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small there exists a constants <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-C_{1}k^{1/2p+\epsilon}&lt;e_{k}(X_{f})&lt;-C_{2}k^{1/2p-\epsilon}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-C_{1}k^{1/2p+\epsilon}&lt;e_{k}(X_{f})&lt;-C_{2}k^{1/2p-\epsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">First we estimate the volume growth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a constant <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}(X_{f})&lt;Cd^{2-2/p-\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}(X_{f})&lt;Cd^{2-2/p-\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span>
Given <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large we have <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)&lt;x^{-p+\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)&lt;x^{-p+\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and so there is a constant with <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)&lt;C(1+x)^{-p+\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)&lt;C(1+x)^{-p+\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We think of <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> as a fibration over the <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> plane with fibers over <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> being disks of area <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\pi|z|^{2})&lt;C(1+\pi|z|^{2})^{-p+\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\pi|z|^{2})&lt;C(1+\pi|z|^{2})^{-p+\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> as before to be the points within <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of the boundary and <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}\subset N_{d}\subset X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}\subset N_{d}\subset X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the points within <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of the boundary of the corresponding fiber. There is a constant <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> (depending on the slope of <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>) such that <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}\subset W_{Cd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}\subset W_{Cd}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence it suffices to bound the volume of the <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The complement of <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is another toric domain, now corresponding to the region under the graph of</p>
<table id="S4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(r)=\pi\max(0,\sqrt{f(r)/\pi}-d)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(r)=\pi\max(0,\sqrt{f(r)/\pi}-d)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(r_{\infty})=\pi d^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(r_{\infty})=\pi d^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{\infty}&lt;Cd^{-2/(p-\epsilon)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\infty}&lt;Cd^{-2/(p-\epsilon)}</annotation></semantics></math> where we again use <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to denote a constant, in this case independent of <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore we have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{vol}(W_{d})=\int_{0}^{r_{\infty}}(f(r)-\pi(\sqrt{f(r)/\pi}-d)^{2})\,dr+\int_{r_{\infty}}^{\infty}f(r)\,dr" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{vol}(W_{d})=\int_{0}^{r_{\infty}}(f(r)-\pi(\sqrt{f(r)/\pi}-d)^{2})\,dr+\int_{r_{\infty}}^{\infty}f(r)\,dr</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\int_{0}^{r_{\infty}}(2\sqrt{\pi}d\sqrt{f}-\pi d^{2})\,dr+\int_{r_{\infty}}^{\infty}f(r)\,dr" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>π</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><msqrt><mi>f</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\int_{0}^{r_{\infty}}(2\sqrt{\pi}d\sqrt{f}-\pi d^{2})\,dr+\int_{r_{\infty}}^{\infty}f(r)\,dr</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;Cd(1+x)^{1-(p-\epsilon)/2}\big|_{0}^{r_{\infty}}-C(1+x)^{1-p+\epsilon}\big|_{r_{\infty}}^{\infty}&lt;Cd^{2-2/p-\epsilon}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;Cd(1+x)^{1-(p-\epsilon)/2}\big|_{0}^{r_{\infty}}-C(1+x)^{1-p+\epsilon}\big|_{r_{\infty}}^{\infty}&lt;Cd^{2-2/p-\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p9.2" class="ltx_p">since <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, as required. (We note that the same proof applies when <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math> but then the integral of <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>f</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{f}</annotation></semantics></math> converges and we obtain growth of order <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.)
 <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large we have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f^{\prime}(n^{1/(p+1)+\epsilon})|\leq 1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f^{\prime}(n^{1/(p+1)+\epsilon})|\leq 1/n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span> We argue by contradiction, and assume <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f^{\prime}(x)|&gt;1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f^{\prime}(x)|&gt;1/n</annotation></semantics></math> on the interval <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n^{1/(p+1)},n^{1/(p+1)+\epsilon}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n^{1/(p+1)},n^{1/(p+1)+\epsilon}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Then the change <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta f</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> over the interval is bounded by</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta f&gt;\frac{1}{n}(n^{1/(p+1)+\epsilon}-n^{1/(p+1)})=n^{\frac{-p}{p+1}}(n^{\epsilon}-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta f&gt;\frac{1}{n}(n^{1/(p+1)+\epsilon}-n^{1/(p+1)})=n^{\frac{-p}{p+1}}(n^{\epsilon}-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, the decay rate of <math id="S4.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> implies that for large <math id="S4.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{-p-\epsilon}&lt;f(x)&lt;x^{-p+\epsilon}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{-p-\epsilon}&lt;f(x)&lt;x^{-p+\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4.12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta f&lt;n^{1/(p+1)(-p+\epsilon)}-n^{(1/(p+1)+\epsilon)(-p-\epsilon)}\\
=n^{\frac{-p}{p+1}}(n^{\epsilon/(p+1)}-n^{-\epsilon(p+1/(p+1)+\epsilon)})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta f&lt;n^{1/(p+1)(-p+\epsilon)}-n^{(1/(p+1)+\epsilon)(-p-\epsilon)}\\
=n^{\frac{-p}{p+1}}(n^{\epsilon/(p+1)}-n^{-\epsilon(p+1/(p+1)+\epsilon)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">This gives a contradiction since when <math id="S4.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is large we have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\epsilon/(p+1)}-n^{-\epsilon(p+1/(p+1)+\epsilon)}&lt;n^{\epsilon}-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\epsilon/(p+1)}-n^{-\epsilon(p+1/(p+1)+\epsilon)}&lt;n^{\epsilon}-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 4.7. ‣ 4.3. Subleading asymptotics and decay rates ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span>
First, Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 4.8. ‣ 4.3. Subleading asymptotics and decay rates ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> together with Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3. The subleading asymptotics of ECH capacities and the Minkowski dimension ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> implies the lower bound on <math id="S4.SS3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X_{f})</annotation></semantics></math>. Second, Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 4.9. ‣ 4.3. Subleading asymptotics and decay rates ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a> together with Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 4.5. ‣ 4.1. The weight expansion and the ECH capacities ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> implies that the <math id="S4.SS3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th term of the weight expansion for <math id="S4.SS3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least</p>
<table id="S4.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n^{1/(p+1)+\epsilon})\geq n^{(1/(p+1)+\epsilon)(-p-\epsilon)}=n^{-\frac{p}{p+1}-\epsilon(p+\frac{1}{p+1})-\epsilon^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n^{1/(p+1)+\epsilon})\geq n^{(1/(p+1)+\epsilon)(-p-\epsilon)}=n^{-\frac{p}{p+1}-\epsilon(p+\frac{1}{p+1})-\epsilon^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p16.2" class="ltx_p">Then, scaling <math id="S4.SS3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> appropriately, Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 4.6. ‣ 4.2. Estimating the subleading asymptotics in terms of the weights ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> gives the result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p18.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of the domains <math id="S4.SS3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> the defining function is equal to <math id="S4.SS3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+x)^{-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+x)^{-p}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can follow the same argument to estimate the subleading asymptotics but now without any order <math id="S4.SS3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> error terms. This gives the following stronger result, which was previously stated as Proposition <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 1.8. ‣ 1.3. The nature of the obstructions and the fractal Weyl law ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a>; we recall the statement:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist positive constants <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-C_{1}k^{1/2p}&lt;e_{k}(X_{p})&lt;-C_{2}k^{1/2p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-C_{1}k^{1/2p}&lt;e_{k}(X_{p})&lt;-C_{2}k^{1/2p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>The main theorems</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove our main results. We begin with the failure of packing stability for all unbounded concave toric domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  (Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the disjoint union of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> equal open balls, with total volume <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{vol}=\mathrm{vol}(\text{int}(X))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>int</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{vol}=\mathrm{vol}(\text{int}(X))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, Lem. 2.4]</cite>, we have</p>
<table id="S5.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(D_{n})=c_{k}(\lambda E(1,n))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(D_{n})=c_{k}(\lambda E(1,n)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\sqrt{\frac{2\mathrm{vol}}{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\sqrt{\frac{2\mathrm{vol}}{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Prop. 15]</cite>,</p>
<table id="S5.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(E(1,n))&gt;-\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(E(1,n))&gt;-\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Hence, by (<a href="#S5.E1" title="In 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>) together with (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) and Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>,
there exist <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(D_{n})=c_{k}(\lambda E(1,n))&gt;c_{k}(\text{int}(X))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>int</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(D_{n})=c_{k}(\lambda E(1,n))&gt;c_{k}(\text{int}(X)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As the ECH capacities of <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> vary continuously under rescaling the balls, we also get a strict inequality for ECH capacities of sufficiently large compact subsets of <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence by the Monotonicity property of ECH capacities, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Prop. 4.11]</cite>, we see that such compact subsets of <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> do not embed in the interior of <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Ellipsoid embedding functions)
</span>A series of works, see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and the references therein, study the “ellipsoid embedding function” <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{X}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{X}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> of a symplectic manifold; in the case where <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold, this is defined to be the the supremum, over <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the symplectic ellipsoid <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\cdot E(1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\cdot E(1,a)</annotation></semantics></math> can be symplectically embedded into <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this literature, one often distinguishes in the finite volume case between the “flexible case”, where the only embedding obstruction is the volume, and the “rigid case”, where there are further obstructions. In all examples studied prior to our work, it has been the case that flexibility holds whenever <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large. The argument for Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> above implies that flexibility for the ellipsoid embedding function never holds for any <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is an unbounded concave toric domain; alternatively, this follows directly from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, since it is known that packing stability by balls holds for any four-dimensional symplectic ellipsoid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our other two main results, Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>,
are
corollaries of the following more general theorem, which is of potentially independent interest. The theorem is a kind of quantified version of our obstruction to packing stability, in terms of the Minkowski dimension. To state the theorem, say that a symplectic manifold <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fully packs</span> <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if the generalized packing number <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Z,1}(M,\omega^{\prime})=1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Z,1}(M,\omega^{\prime})=1.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> have decay rate <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;p&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;p&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a either a relatively compact and open subset of a symplectic manifold, or an open subset of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> fully packs <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the inner Minkowski dimension of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> must be at least <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+2/p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+2/p</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We recall from section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1. Volume decay and the Minkowski dimension ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> that the inner Minkowski dimension is well defined in these situations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Before proving Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we explain why it implies the remaining main results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  (Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>, assuming Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>)</span>
Let <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z,\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z,\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be as in the statement of that theorem and let <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the disjoint union of <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z,\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z,\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y_{k},1}(X_{f})=p_{Z,k}(X_{f})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y_{k},1}(X_{f})=p_{Z,k}(X_{f}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, the inner Minkowski dimension of <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z,\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z,\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>: in the case where <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is compact with smooth boundary, this follows from Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.1. Volume decay and the Minkowski dimension ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, and in the case where <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a convex domain this follows from Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2.1. Volume decay and the Minkowski dimension ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>. Thus the inner Minkowski dimension of <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, and so by Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> and (<a href="#S5.E3" title="In 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Z,k}(X_{f})&lt;1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Z,k}(X_{f})&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p10.3" class="ltx_p">hence Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>.
 <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  (Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, assuming Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S5.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f})</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to <math id="S5.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="S5.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> fully packs <math id="S5.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus by Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> the inner Minkowski dimension of <math id="S5.SS1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="S5.SS1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+2/p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+2/p</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="S5.SS1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We now give the promised proof of our quantified variant of the failure of packing stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  (Proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> be the order of the volume decay near <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Y</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the inner Minkowski dimension of <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4-q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4-q</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> be such that <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,c\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,c\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> has the same volume as <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3. The subleading asymptotics of ECH capacities and the Minkowski dimension ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, or by Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3. The subleading asymptotics of ECH capacities and the Minkowski dimension ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> in the Euclidean case,</p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(Y,c\omega)&gt;-Ck^{(2-q)/4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(Y,c\omega)&gt;-Ck^{(2-q)/4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p15.2" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, by Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a></p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(X_{p})&lt;-C_{1}k^{1/2p-\epsilon}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(X_{p})&lt;-C_{1}k^{1/2p-\epsilon},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p15.3" class="ltx_p">with the constant <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> depending on <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus if</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4-q&lt;2+2/p" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4-q&lt;2+2/p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p15.4" class="ltx_p">then for <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small</p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-C_{1}k^{1/2p-\epsilon}&lt;-Ck^{(2-q)/4}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-C_{1}k^{1/2p-\epsilon}&lt;-Ck^{(2-q)/4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p15.5" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large, hence, in combination with (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>) and (<a href="#S5.E5" title="In 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(Y,c\omega)&gt;c_{k}(X_{p})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(Y,c\omega)&gt;c_{k}(X_{p})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p15.6" class="ltx_p">for some <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, by the Scaling and Monotonicity properties, <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,c^{\prime}\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,c^{\prime}\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> does not embed into <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> for any scaling factor <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the volume is sufficiently close to the volume of <math id="S5.SS1.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so the theorem is proved.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Some further applications around Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> is more general than our main theorems, so we now give some examples of some further applications beyond our main results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Infinite packings)
</span>Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. The main theorems ‣ 5. A quantified theorem and proofs of the main results ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> does not require the domain to have even finitely many components. For example, we can study infinite ball packings.
The <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, being unbounded concave toric domains, have a canonical ball-packing; however, we can obstruct many other ball-packings. For infinite ball packings, the inner Minkowski dimension is easy to compute, and in general we find that for balls <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sqcup_{i\geq 1}B(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup_{i\geq 1}B(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> to fully fill an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f})</annotation></semantics></math> with decay rate <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;p&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;p&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, the partial sums <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum^{k}_{i=1}a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum^{k}_{i=1}a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> must grow sufficiently fast. To give just one example, let
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y=\sqcup_{i\geq 1}B(i^{-q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\sqcup_{i\geq 1}B(i^{-q})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2&lt;q&lt;2/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2&lt;q&lt;2/3</annotation></semantics></math>. One can compute that this has inner Minkowski dimension <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2/q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2/q</annotation></semantics></math> and so the theorem implies the disjoint union fully fills an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> with decay rate <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> only if <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&lt;\frac{p}{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&lt;\frac{p}{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Unbounded concave domains with different decay rates)
</span>Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{f_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{f_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> be unbounded concave toric domains, such that the decay rate of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, the decay rate of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;p&lt;q&lt;2." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;p&lt;q&lt;2.</annotation></semantics></math> Then, it follows from
that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> does not fully fill <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f_{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math>, since
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> has
inner Minkowski dimension <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+2/q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+2/q</annotation></semantics></math>, and this is smaller than <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+2/p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+2/p</annotation></semantics></math>; alternatively, we can apply Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 4.7. ‣ 4.3. Subleading asymptotics and decay rates ‣ 4. The subleading asymptotics of unbounded concave toric domains ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>. It would be interesting to know if it must be the case that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f_{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math> can fully fill <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="int(X_{f_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">int(X_{f_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Boundedness.</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we address Proposition <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 1.3. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>. More generally, we show that a large class of toric domains <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> (including concave toric domains) of finite volume are symplectomorphic to the interior of compact subsets of <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. At least in the case of the <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> we show the boundary of these compact sets have the same inner Minkowski dimension as the <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> themselves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use coordinates <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z,w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z,w</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}\equiv\operatorname{\mathbb{R}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≡</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}\equiv\operatorname{\mathbb{R}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=x+iy,w=u+iv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=x+iy,w=u+iv</annotation></semantics></math>. The round disk of area <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonincreasing function with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(0)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(0)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\int_{0}^{\infty}f(r)\,dr&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\int_{0}^{\infty}f(r)\,dr&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega=\{s\leq f(r)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=\{s\leq f(r)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=X_{\Omega}=\{(z,w)|(\pi|z|^{2},\pi|w|^{2})\in\Omega\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=X_{\Omega}=\{(z,w)|(\pi|z|^{2},\pi|w|^{2})\in\Omega\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a symplectic embedding</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:X\hookrightarrow D(2+\delta)\times D(V+2)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:X\hookrightarrow D(2+\delta)\times D(V+2).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover:</span></p>
<ol id="S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the interior of the compact set </span><math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{F(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{F(X)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the case when </span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the volume decay rate of
</span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is equal to that of </span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We note there exist toric domains <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of finite volume but which do not admit symplectic embeddings into any bounded set. For example we can take <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=X_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=X_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is an open neighborhood of the diagonal <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{r=s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r=s\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The first examples of this nature were due to Hermann, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.  </span> The proof adapts the symplectic folding construction to a form more sensitive to the Minkowski dimension.
The construction of the embedding has 3 steps. In each step we define a symplectic embedding, <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, and their images <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=\Phi(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=\Phi(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=\Psi(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=\Psi(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and our bounded domain <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{3}=\Xi(X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{3}=\Xi(X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. The construction will show that <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> have bounded derivatives (unlike <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>). Hence to estimate the inner Minkowski dimension it suffices to look at <math id="S6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so we describe this set in detail.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 1. Preparation. The embedding <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal here is to map our manifold <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a fibration over the <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> plane <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> with fibers <math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(f(\pi|z|^{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(f(\pi|z|^{2}))</annotation></semantics></math>, to a fibration over a strip <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We start with the region</p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(0,\infty)\times(0,1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(0,\infty)\times(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p6.2" class="ltx_p">in the first quadrant of the <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> plane.
Let <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic embedding of the <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> plane into itself with image <math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can choose the embedding such that all disks <math id="S6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(R)</annotation></semantics></math> map into a neighborhood of the region <math id="S6.p6.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x&lt;R\}\cap T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x&lt;R\}\cap T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here we note that the regions <math id="S6.p6.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x&lt;R\}\cap T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x&lt;R\}\cap T</annotation></semantics></math> have area <math id="S6.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and so such an embedding is possible by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, Lemma 3.1.5. We arrange that when <math id="S6.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S&gt;R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S&gt;R</annotation></semantics></math> the image <math id="S6.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}(D(S)\setminus D(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}(D(S)\setminus D(R))</annotation></semantics></math> intersects <math id="S6.p6.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x&lt;R\}\cap T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x&lt;R\}\cap T</annotation></semantics></math> only in a very small neighborhood of its boundary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectomorphism of the <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> plane mapping the disks <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(R)</annotation></semantics></math> to approximations of squares <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, of side length <math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>R</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, centered at the origin. Again we can construct such a symplectomorphism using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, Lemma 3.1.5.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Now we define <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi=\phi_{1}\times\phi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=\phi_{1}\times\phi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We think of the image <math id="S6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=\Phi(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=\Phi(X)</annotation></semantics></math> as fibered over <math id="S6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that the set is an approximation (in a sense we describe momentarily) of the set <math id="S6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is the fibration over <math id="S6.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with fibers <math id="S6.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> then is a manifold with corners, and <math id="S6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> itself will have smooth boundary, smoothly approximating the smooth parts of <math id="S6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\tilde{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\tilde{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and rounding the corners.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p">To see the approximation, suppose <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=R</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in\phi_{1}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in\phi_{1}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> is away from the boundary . Then <math id="S6.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is disjoint from <math id="S6.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}(D(S))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}(D(S))</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is slightly smaller than <math id="S6.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, but if <math id="S6.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> lies away from <math id="S6.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\phi_{1}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\phi_{1}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> then it also avoids the images of points with <math id="S6.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|z|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|z|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> greater than <math id="S6.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S6.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)=\phi_{1}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)=\phi_{1}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|z|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|z|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is approximately <math id="S6.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, and so the fiber must approximate <math id="S6.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{2}(D(f(R)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{2}(D(f(R)))</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(f(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(f(R))</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can choose this approximation as close as necessary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Points <math id="S6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=R</annotation></semantics></math> and close to <math id="S6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\phi_{1}(\mathbb{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\phi_{1}(\mathbb{C})</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the image of a point with <math id="S6.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|z|^{2}&gt;R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|z|^{2}&gt;R</annotation></semantics></math>, and we can arrange <math id="S6.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|z|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|z|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to increase rapidly, and hence the size of the fiber to decrease rapidly, as we approach the boundary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 2. Displacing fibers. The embedding <math id="S6.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to describe a domain <math id="S6.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=\Psi(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=\Psi(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> with the property that the <math id="S6.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> fibers over points <math id="S6.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},y_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},y_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{2},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{2},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S6.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> plane with <math id="S6.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x_{1}-x_{2}|\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x_{1}-x_{2}|\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint. We will do this by translating the squares <math id="S6.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S6.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> direction, as we describe now.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 2a. Moving the squares.</span>
Let</p>
<table id="S6.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(w)=v+1/2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(w)=v+1/2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p14.2" class="ltx_p">Then as <math id="S6.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S6.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq G\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq G\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Hamiltonian vector field <math id="S6.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{G}=\frac{\partial}{\partial u}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{G}=\frac{\partial}{\partial u}.</annotation></semantics></math> The flow is time independent and displaces a square <math id="S6.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(R)</annotation></semantics></math> in time <math id="S6.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>R</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 2b. Displacing the fibers.</span>
Fix a function <math id="S6.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(x)=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(x)=x</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}(x)=\sqrt{f(x-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}(x)=\sqrt{f(x-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. We recall our normalization <math id="S6.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(0)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(0)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Then we consider the Hamiltonian diffeomorphism <math id="S6.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> generated by</p>
<table id="S6.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(z,w)=\chi(x)G(w)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(z,w)=\chi(x)G(w).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p17.1" class="ltx_p">To study the effect of <math id="S6.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> we apply the product rule to write</p>
<table id="S6.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{H}(x,y,w)=\chi(x)X_{G}(w)+G(w)\chi^{\prime}(x)\frac{\partial}{\partial y}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{H}(x,y,w)=\chi(x)X_{G}(w)+G(w)\chi^{\prime}(x)\frac{\partial}{\partial y}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p17.2" class="ltx_p">We observe that <math id="S6.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the <math id="S6.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> coordinate, and as <math id="S6.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an integral of the motion the <math id="S6.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial y}</annotation></semantics></math> component is independent of time. This implies that <math id="S6.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as a composition <math id="S6.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{z}\circ\Psi^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>z</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>w</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{z}\circ\Psi^{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{z}</annotation></semantics></math> fixes the <math id="S6.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> coordinate and translates in the <math id="S6.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> direction by <math id="S6.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(w)\chi^{\prime}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(w)\chi^{\prime}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S6.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>w</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{w}</annotation></semantics></math> fixes the <math id="S6.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> coordinate and flows along <math id="S6.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> for time <math id="S6.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p18.1" class="ltx_p">We have</p>
<table id="S6.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{z}(x,y,u,v)=(x,y+(v+1/2)\chi^{\prime}(x),u,v)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>z</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{z}(x,y,u,v)=(x,y+(v+1/2)\chi^{\prime}(x),u,v).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p18.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, as <math id="S6.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq G=v+1/2\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq G=v+1/2\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that the translation <math id="S6.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonnegative and bounded by <math id="S6.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}(x)\leq 1+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}(x)\leq 1+\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the projection of <math id="S6.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=\Psi(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=\Psi(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S6.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> plane now lies in a strip</p>
<table id="S6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}=(0,\infty)\times(0,2+\delta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}=(0,\infty)\times(0,2+\delta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p19.1" class="ltx_p">The diffeomorphism</p>
<table id="S6.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{w}(x,y,u,v)=(x,y,u+\chi(x),v)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>w</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{w}(x,y,u,v)=(x,y,u+\chi(x),v).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p19.2" class="ltx_p">Like <math id="S6.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the image <math id="S6.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{w}_{2}=\Psi^{w}(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>w</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{w}_{2}=\Psi^{w}(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> also fibers over <math id="S6.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, but now the fiber over a point <math id="S6.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is the square <math id="S6.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(f(x))+\chi(x)e_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(f(x))+\chi(x)e_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{u}=(0,0,1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{u}=(0,0,1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> is the unit vector in the <math id="S6.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> direction.
Our condition on <math id="S6.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S6.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(x)-\chi(x-1)&gt;\sqrt{f(x-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(x)-\chi(x-1)&gt;\sqrt{f(x-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the width of the fiber over points <math id="S6.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x-1,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x-1,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence the fibers of <math id="S6.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{w}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{w}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> over points <math id="S6.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},y_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},y_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> are now disjoint from those over points <math id="S6.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{2},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{2},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x_{1}-x_{2}|\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x_{1}-x_{2}|\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. These fibers remain disjoint if we apply <math id="S6.p19.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{z}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence we also have that the fibers of <math id="S6.p19.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=\Psi(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=\Psi(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> over points <math id="S6.p19.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},y_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},y_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p19.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{2},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{2},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p19.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x_{1}-x_{2}|\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x_{1}-x_{2}|\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint.
It is clear that both <math id="S6.p19.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{z}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{z}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p19.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{w}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{w}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> have uniformly bounded derivatives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p20.1" class="ltx_p">In preparation for the next step, we compute the area of the projection <math id="S6.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}^{w}</annotation></semantics></math> (or equivalently <math id="S6.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) onto the <math id="S6.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> plane. For this, if <math id="S6.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection of <math id="S6.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{w}(x,y,u^{\prime},v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>w</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{w}(x,y,u^{\prime},v)</annotation></semantics></math> then, as <math id="S6.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u^{\prime},v)\in S(f(1))=S(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u^{\prime},v)\in S(f(1))=S(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S6.p20.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u^{\prime}\in[-1/2,1/2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}\in[-1/2,1/2]</annotation></semantics></math>, formula <a href="#S6.E2" title="In 6. Boundedness. ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> gives <math id="S6.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(x)\in[u-1/2,u+1/2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(x)\in[u-1/2,u+1/2]</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, if <math id="S6.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\geq 1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\geq 1/2</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S6.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> coordinate lies in an interval of length at most <math id="S6.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{f(\chi^{-1}(u-1/2))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{f(\chi^{-1}(u-1/2))}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S6.p20.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u\in[-1/2,1/2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in[-1/2,1/2]</annotation></semantics></math> we still have <math id="S6.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in an interval of length <math id="S6.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the area of <math id="S6.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded by</p>
<table id="S6.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}1+\int_{1/2}^{\infty}\sqrt{f(\chi^{-1}(u-\frac{1}{2}))}\,du&amp;=1+\int_{0}^{\infty}\sqrt{f(y)}\chi^{\prime}(y)\,dy\\
&amp;&lt;2+\int_{1}^{\infty}f(y-1)\,dy\leq V+2.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}1+\int_{1/2}^{\infty}\sqrt{f(\chi^{-1}(u-\frac{1}{2}))}\,du&amp;=1+\int_{0}^{\infty}\sqrt{f(y)}\chi^{\prime}(y)\,dy\\
&amp;&lt;2+\int_{1}^{\infty}f(y-1)\,dy\leq V+2.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p20.2" class="ltx_p">where we use the identity <math id="S6.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}(y)=\sqrt{f(y-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}(y)=\sqrt{f(y-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and recall <math id="S6.p20.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(y)\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(y)\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 3. Wrapping.</span> To complete the description of our embedding let <math id="S6.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be an immersion of the region <math id="S6.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}=(0,\infty)\times(0,2+\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}=(0,\infty)\times(0,2+\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S6.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-plane with image contained in <math id="S6.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(2+2\delta)\setminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(2+2\delta)\setminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and having the property that <math id="S6.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{arg}\xi_{1}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{arg}\xi_{1}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> depends only upon <math id="S6.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, vertical line segments map to rays. We arrange that points <math id="S6.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},y_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},y_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{2},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{2},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> map to the same ray if and only if <math id="S6.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}-x_{2}\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}-x_{2}\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Concretely, we can choose polar coordinates <math id="S6.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\pi|z|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\pi|z|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> on the plane, and set <math id="S6.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}(x,y)=(R,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}(x,y)=(R,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=y+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=y+\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=-x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectomorphism of the <math id="S6.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> plane mapping the region <math id="S6.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> into the disk <math id="S6.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(V+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(V+2)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is possible by the calculation at the end of Step 2, and for the purposes of the current paper our concern is just that the disk has finite area. The map can be chosen to wind the infinite strip <math id="S6.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> continuously around the origin, mapping points with <math id="S6.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> large close to the boundary of the disk.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p23.1" class="ltx_p">To be concrete, we recall that <math id="S6.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the region</p>
<table id="S6.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left[\frac{-1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right]\times\left[\frac{-1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right]\bigcup\{u\geq 1/2,\,|v|&lt;\sqrt{f(\chi^{-1}(u-1/2))}/2\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">&lt;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[\frac{-1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right]\times\left[\frac{-1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right]\bigcup\{u\geq 1/2,\,|v|&lt;\sqrt{f(\chi^{-1}(u-1/2))}/2\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p23.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p23.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma:[-1/2,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:[-1/2,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S6.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(u)=u+1/2+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(u)=u+1/2+\delta</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\leq 1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\leq 1/2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(u)=\sqrt{f(\chi^{-1}(u-1/2))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(u)=\sqrt{f(\chi^{-1}(u-1/2))}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\geq 1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\geq 1/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then using polar coordinates as before we can define <math id="S6.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{2}(u,v)=(R,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{2}(u,v)=(R,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\sigma(u)+v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\sigma(u)+v</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=-u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=-u</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives a symplectic embedding of <math id="S6.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> into a disk of area</p>
<table id="S6.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{u\to\infty}\sigma(u)=\delta+1+\int_{1/2}^{\infty}\sigma^{\prime}(u)\,du&lt;V+2" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{u\to\infty}\sigma(u)=\delta+1+\int_{1/2}^{\infty}\sigma^{\prime}(u)\,du&lt;V+2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p23.3" class="ltx_p">by the calculation at the end of Step 2.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p24.1" class="ltx_p">We see that both <math id="S6.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have bounded derivatives. We define <math id="S6.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi=\xi_{1}\times\xi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi=\xi_{1}\times\xi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="S6.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{3}=\Xi(X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{3}=\Xi(X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 4. Injectivity.</span> We note that any double points <math id="S6.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> necessarily have <math id="S6.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x-x^{\prime}\in\mathbb{Z}\setminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x-x^{\prime}\in\mathbb{Z}\setminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence the fibers of <math id="S6.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> over such points are disjoint. Therefore the map <math id="S6.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedding of <math id="S6.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=\Xi\circ\Psi\circ\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=\Xi\circ\Psi\circ\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> gives an embedding of <math id="S6.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, with image lying inside <math id="S6.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(2+2\delta)\times D(V+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(2+2\delta)\times D(V+2)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular <math id="S6.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to a bounded domain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 5. Interior of a compact set.</span> Having described our embedding <math id="S6.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, and proved part of the proposition, we now prove the rest. For the second statement in Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 6.1. ‣ 6. Boundedness. ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> we study the bounded open set <math id="S6.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X_{3}=F(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X_{3}=F(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and claim that <math id="S6.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is the interior of <math id="S6.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, we need to show that points in <math id="S6.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Y</annotation></semantics></math> do not lie in the interior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p27.1" class="ltx_p">In fact we can describe <math id="S6.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Y</annotation></semantics></math> explicitly: it is a union of two sets <math id="S6.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}\cup Z_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}\cup Z_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> we define now.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p28.1" class="ltx_p">The embedding <math id="S6.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi\circ\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi\circ\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> extends to an embedding of an open neighborhood of <math id="S6.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we set <math id="S6.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}=\Xi\circ\Psi(\partial X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}=\Xi\circ\Psi(\partial X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. We recall <math id="S6.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is unbounded but arranged to have smooth boundary. As <math id="S6.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi\circ\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi\circ\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> extends smoothly across <math id="S6.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, points in <math id="S6.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are also in the boundary of <math id="S6.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p29.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder <math id="S6.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial Y</annotation></semantics></math> consists of accumulation points <math id="S6.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{\infty},w_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{\infty},w_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math> of sequences <math id="S6.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi\circ\Psi(z_{i},w_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi\circ\Psi(z_{i},w_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{i},w_{i})\in X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{i},w_{i})\in X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Re}z_{i}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Re}z_{i}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, such points have <math id="S6.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|w_{\infty}|^{2}=\lim_{u\to\infty}\sigma(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|w_{\infty}|^{2}=\lim_{u\to\infty}\sigma(u)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p29.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is the map described in Step 3. This limit is a finite number <math id="S6.p29.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l&lt;V+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l&lt;V+2</annotation></semantics></math>, and as any neighborhood of <math id="S6.p29.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{\infty},w_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{\infty},w_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math> contains points with <math id="S6.p29.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|w|^{2}&gt;l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|w|^{2}&gt;l</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that <math id="S6.p29.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is also contained in the boundary of <math id="S6.p29.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> as required.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark.</span> As an aside we note (at least in the case when the integral of <math id="S6.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>f</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{f}</annotation></semantics></math> diverges, that is, when <math id="S6.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is unbounded) that the set <math id="S6.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is actually precisely the product</p>
<table id="S6.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{2}=\{\delta\leq\pi|z|^{2}\leq 1+\delta\}\times\{\pi|w|^{2}=l\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{2}=\{\delta\leq\pi|z|^{2}\leq 1+\delta\}\times\{\pi|w|^{2}=l\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p30.2" class="ltx_p">Indeed, when <math id="S6.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\mathrm{Re}z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\mathrm{Re}z</annotation></semantics></math> is large, <math id="S6.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is small and the map <math id="S6.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>z</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{z}</annotation></semantics></math> from formula <a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6. Boundedness. ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> is small. Hence if <math id="S6.p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is large the <math id="S6.p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> fiber of <math id="S6.p30.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=\Psi(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=\Psi(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> over a point <math id="S6.p30.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is a square <math id="S6.p30.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(f(x))+\chi(x)e_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(f(x))+\chi(x)e_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p30.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;y&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;y&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and empty otherwise (up to small perturbations which decrease with <math id="S6.p30.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>). In particular, applying <math id="S6.p30.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.p30.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{2}\cap\{x&gt;K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}\cap\{x&gt;K\}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p30.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is large, the <math id="S6.p30.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> projection of the image approximates the image of the map <math id="S6.p30.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> applied to <math id="S6.p30.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x&gt;K,\,0&lt;y&lt;1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x&gt;K,\,0&lt;y&lt;1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the annulus <math id="S6.p30.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\delta&lt;\pi|z|^{2}&lt;1+\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\delta&lt;\pi|z|^{2}&lt;1+\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S6.p30.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the set described.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p31.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, fix <math id="S6.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq r&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq r&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;y&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;y&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the sequence of points <math id="S6.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{n}=n+r+iy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{n}=n+r+iy</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the map <math id="S6.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from Step 3, all <math id="S6.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are mapped to <math id="S6.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}(z_{n})=\xi_{1}(r+iy)=(R=y+\delta,\theta=-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}(z_{n})=\xi_{1}(r+iy)=(R=y+\delta,\theta=-r)</annotation></semantics></math>. Acting on the fiber of <math id="S6.p31.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S6.p31.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a square centered at <math id="S6.p31.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\chi(n+r),0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\chi(n+r),0)</annotation></semantics></math>, the argument of <math id="S6.p31.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> forms an interval around <math id="S6.p31.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=-\chi(n+r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=-\chi(n+r)</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S6.p31.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> is unbounded but has derivative decreasing to <math id="S6.p31.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, these points form a dense set of arguments as <math id="S6.p31.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> increases, showing that all points in the circle <math id="S6.p31.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\pi|w|^{2}=l\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pi|w|^{2}=l\}</annotation></semantics></math> appear as accumulation points of the fiber of <math id="S6.p31.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S6.p31.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}(r+iy)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}(r+iy)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 6. Minkowski dimension.</span> To conclude the proof of Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 6.1. ‣ 6. Boundedness. ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> we carry out some estimates in the case when <math id="S6.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, when <math id="S6.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=(1+x)^{-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=(1+x)^{-p}</annotation></semantics></math>. We focus on the case when <math id="S6.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;p&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;p&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, though the same argument works when <math id="S6.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p33.1" class="ltx_p">As the maps <math id="S6.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> have uniformly bounded derivatives, the Minkowski dimension of <math id="S6.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(X)</annotation></semantics></math> will be equal to that of <math id="S6.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=\Phi(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=\Phi(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. The nature of our approximation implies that this is also the Minkowski dimension of <math id="S6.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p34.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the points within <math id="S6.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of the boundary of <math id="S6.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This contains all square fibers of side length less than <math id="S6.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, the fibers over points with <math id="S6.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&gt;x_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&gt;x_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{f(x_{\infty})}=2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{f(x_{\infty})}=2d</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p34.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{\infty}=Cd^{-2/p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\infty}=Cd^{-2/p}</annotation></semantics></math> (throughout <math id="S6.p34.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a constant independent of <math id="S6.p34.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>). The volume of the total space of these fibers is then</p>
<table id="S6.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{x_{\infty}}^{\infty}f(x)\,dx=Cd^{2-2/p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{x_{\infty}}^{\infty}f(x)\,dx=Cd^{2-2/p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p35.1" class="ltx_p">Next we consider the fibers over points with <math id="S6.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&lt;x_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&lt;x_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. The set <math id="S6.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is now contained in a union of four subsets:</p>
<ol id="S6.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">fibers over points with <math id="S6.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">fibers over points with <math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">fibers over points with <math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&gt;1-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&gt;1-d</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">points <math id="S6.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,w)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in S(f(x))\setminus S(g(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in S(f(x))\setminus S(g(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(g(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(g(x))</annotation></semantics></math> is a square of side length <math id="S6.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{f(x)}-2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{f(x)}-2d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S6.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p36.1" class="ltx_p">Set (1) has volume bounded by <math id="S6.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="df(0)=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">df(0)=d</annotation></semantics></math>. Sets (2) and (3) have volume bounded by</p>
<table id="S6.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\int_{0}^{x_{\infty}}f(x)\,dx&lt;Cd." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\int_{0}^{x_{\infty}}f(x)\,dx&lt;Cd.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p36.2" class="ltx_p">Finally set (4) has volume bounded by</p>
<table id="S6.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{x_{\infty}}(f(x)-g(x))\,dx=\int_{0}^{x_{\infty}}(2d\sqrt{f(x)}-4d^{2})&lt;Cd^{2-2/p}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{x_{\infty}}(f(x)-g(x))\,dx=\int_{0}^{x_{\infty}}(2d\sqrt{f(x)}-4d^{2})&lt;Cd^{2-2/p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p36.3" class="ltx_p">using <math id="S6.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)=(\sqrt{f(x)}-2d)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)=(\sqrt{f(x)}-2d)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p37.1" class="ltx_p">Putting the estimates together gives our Minkowski dimension as required.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p38.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given our Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, and the context surveyed in the introduction, it is natural to now revisit the question of which finite volume symplectic manifolds have packing stability.
In view of the positive results for closed manifolds surveyed in the introduction, the natural guess would be that all closed manifolds do.
On the other hand, as we have shown here, packing stability can fail for non-compact manifolds even though it sometimes holds in this class. One would like to get a better handle on the interface between these phenomena: a natural question is what properties of a non-compact manifold guarantee that packing stability holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the cases presented here, the boundary behavior certainly appears to be playing a key role.
Whether the singular character of the boundary in our examples is necessary for packing stability to fail seems a natural starting point for further study. Here is a sample question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Must packing stability hold for every compact Liouville domain with smooth boundary?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All known non-compact examples for which packing stability holds have quite a lot more structure than this. In this regard, let us also note that our obstruction here comes from the divergence of the subleading asymptotics of ECH capacities for our domains. However, various conjectures imply that in sufficiently nice cases these should converge; see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Conj. 1.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In a different direction, one would like to know if analogues of our results holds in higher dimensions. The perspective conveyed in Remark <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Remark 1.6. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> certainly seems like it should hold in arbitrary dimension, but the obstructions we use here come from ECH capacities, which do not currently have a good generalization in higher dimensions.
Thus the following two basic questions remain open:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 7.2</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can packing stability fail in dimension <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;4</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Are there “boundaryless” discs in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>? More precisely, do there exist bounded and open subsets of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, diffeomorphic to discs, that can not be symplectomorphic to the interior of any compact symplectic manifold?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It would also be interesting if one can improve on the condition <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Boundaries of open symplectic manifolds and the failure of packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>, for example with respect to packings by smooth domains: this would follow immediately from our results if one had better lower bounds on the growth rate of the subleading asymptotics of ECH capacities of smooth compact manifolds, which is an interesting problem of independent interest.</p>
</div>
</section>
